/* The header for this test is at the BOTTOM of this file, below the script.
   It is there because a constant inside a leading comment can swallow the
   rest of the file in the engine - see RMSTEST_56 and the directory README.
   Nothing below the script can do any harm. */

<PLAYER_SETUP>
  grouped_by_team

<LAND_GENERATION>
  base_terrain GRASS

  create_player_lands
  {
    terrain_type GRASS2
    base_size 20
    number_of_tiles 200
    circle_radius 40 0
  }

<ELEVATION_GENERATION>


<TERRAIN_GENERATION>


<OBJECTS_GENERATION>


<CONNECTION_GENERATION>


/*
================================================================================
RMSTEST_79 - what does grouped_by_team do when the packed groups do not fit
the ring's circumference?
Feeds: docs/preview-design.md Sec.6.1 line ~245 ("Still unmeasured: what
happens when the packed groups do not fit the circumference, reachable on
a small map with a large base_size"), src/preview/generator/lands.ts
(grouped_by_team origin placement)
================================================================================

THE QUESTION
Sec.6.1 models grouped_by_team as: divide the ring by GROUP count, lay each
group's members along the ring centred on its slot, spaced roughly
3.5-4x base_size apart (RMSTEST_36's own measured figure, itself only
"the working figure [tune]" since it never had reason to be pushed to its
limit). Nothing says what happens when four groups each demanding a wide
intra-team arc cannot all fit around one ring at once. Three genuinely
different engine behaviours are all consistent with everything measured
so far:
  (a) CLAMP - the engine compresses intra-team spacing to whatever fits,
      keeping the ring radius and every group non-overlapping.
  (b) OVERLAP - the engine keeps the declared/implied spacing and lets
      origins collide, land stamps overlap, or later lands overwrite
      earlier ones (the same failure shape Sec.15 item 5 already
      documents for ordinary land placement - "a LATER land's stamp
      overwrote an EARLIER one's origin square entirely").
  (c) EXPAND - the engine silently grows the ring radius beyond what
      circle_radius declares so everyone fits.

METHOD
Eight players, four teams of two, grouped_by_team, on a TINY (120-tile)
map with an explicit, variance-free circle_radius 40 0 (RMSTEST_36's own
calibration - a perfect, deterministic ring with no radial or angular
jitter, so every deviation is attributable to grouped_by_team's own
packing logic and nothing else) and a deliberately oversized base_size 20.
On Tiny, radius 40% of dim = 48 tiles, ring circumference approximately
2*pi*48 = 301.6 tiles, divided four ways among the teams leaves about 75
tiles of arc per team. RMSTEST_36's ~3.5-4x base_size spacing figure at
base_size 20 predicts roughly 70-80 tiles needed just between the TWO
members of one team - consuming an entire team's own arc allotment before
any gap between teams is accounted for, and four teams each doing this
cannot all fit on a 301.6-tile ring with anything left over.

SETTING UP TEAMS
RMSTEST_36 already established the scenario editor's Random Map generation
screen can assign teams (a 4v4 came back as a correct [1,3,5,7] vs
[2,4,6,8] split). Set four teams of two: players (1,2), (3,4), (5,6),
(7,8) - any consistent pairing works, since Sec.3.1's canonicalisation to
lobby order is what actually matters, not which literal pair.

PREDICTIONS
  CLAMP -> all 8 lands generate as 8 separate, non-overlapping patches;
    measured intra-team spacing comes in noticeably BELOW RMSTEST_36's
    ~3.5-4x base_size figure - i.e. below ~70-80 tiles here - while
    teammates are still clearly closer to each other than to any
    non-teammate.
  OVERLAP -> fewer than 8 distinct GRASS2 patches (teammates merge into
    one blob under 4-connectivity), or `originFallbackCenter`-style
    clustering near the map's true centre.
  EXPAND -> the measured origin-to-map-centre radius comes out well above
    48 tiles (0.40 * dim) for some or all players, i.e. the ring itself
    grew rather than the spacing shrinking.

GENERATE AT TINY (120), 8 PLAYERS, 4 TEAMS OF 2. THREE RUNS.

READ WITH
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --patches GRASS2
  python probe_scenario.py "<scenario>" --bbox GRASS2
  Count patches first (8 vs fewer settles OVERLAP outright). For each
  patch, read its centroid, compute its distance from the map's true
  centre (60, 60 on a 120-tile map) against the declared 48-tile radius,
  and compute pairwise centroid distances within each declared team
  against RMSTEST_36's ~3.5-4x base_size benchmark (70-80 tiles here).
================================================================================
*/
